INT. MATTRESS SHOWROOM - DAY

Forty beds under bright lights. Banners reading SLEEP BETTER, LIVE BETTER. A wall of pillows. Soft jazz.

REGINALD "REG" PAPADOPOULOS, fifties, a salesman in a rumpled blazer, lies on a king-sized display bed with his hands folded on his chest, eyes shut. Across the aisle, GRETCHEN SUNDQVIST, thirties, an assistant manager with a tablet, taps without looking up. Between them, on a queen mattress, OSWIN TREMBLAY, seventy, a customer in a flat cap, sits and bounces gently, testing.

OSWIN
This one has a nice give.

GRETCHEN
It's our Premium Plus.

OSWIN
What's the premium?

GRETCHEN
The plus.

OSWIN
(to himself)
Mm.

He lies down beside Reg, who doesn't move. They lie shoulder to shoulder, staring at the ceiling.

REG
(whispering)
Don't tell her I'm off the clock.

OSWIN
I thought you were the salesman.

REG
I'm a man in bed.

GRETCHEN
(without looking)
Reg. Customer.

REG
(opening one eye)
I'm helping him.

GRETCHEN
You're asleep.

REG
I'm demonstrating.

GRETCHEN
(to the showroom at large)
We offer a hundred-night trial on all models.

OSWIN
A hundred nights?

REG
(softly)
Don't do it.

OSWIN
Why?

REG
Because you'll sleep.

Oswin considers this for a long moment. He turns his head toward Reg, and Reg turns toward him.

OSWIN
I'll take it.

GRETCHEN
(still tapping)
Excellent.

Neither of them gets up.
